          "metadata": {
            "modelConfiguration": {
              "modelProperties": {
                "k": 0,
                "max_completion_tokens": 32000,
                "reasoning_effort": "low"
              },
              "code": "ORA_MODEL_CONFIG_PREMIUM_OPEN_AI_GPT_4_1_MINI",
              "model": "OCI_GPT_5_MINI",
              "modelName": "Gpt-5 Mini",
              "provider": "CERT_PREMIUM"
            },
            "name": "Generate Config Rule Description LLM"
          },
          "outcomes": {
            "success": "end"
          },
          "code": "GENERATE_CONFIG_RULE_DESCRIPTION_LLM",
          "convergenceTargetId": "1772481473897",
          "id": "1770663963248",
          "inputs": [
            {
              "id": "1770663963250_1",
              "name": "prompt",
              "type": "string",
              "value": "You are an expert Oracle CPQ administrator and a prompt-writing specialist. You are given a JSON object {{$context.$triggers.REST.$input.ruleInstance}} that represents a configuration rule. Produce a concise but **descriptive** 1–3 sentence plain-text summary of the rule in this exact format:\\n\\n    <ExactRuleTypeFromJSON>: [Sentence 1] [Sentence 2 if needed]\\n\\nImportant: Use the rule type **exactly** as provided in the JSON field `ruleType` (case and wording unchanged). Do not modify, normalize, or translate the ruleType in any way. If `ruleType` is missing, use the literal text `Rule` as a fallback.\\n\\nGeneral formatting rules\\n- Always start the output with the exact `ruleType` value followed by a colon and a single space.\\n- Always wrap **attribute names** in ASCII straight double quotes `\\\"` (for example `\\\"ProductType\\\"`).\\n- Use ASCII straight double quotes only — never use typographic (curly) quotes.\\n- Output must be plain text only — no JSON, markdown, or extra commentary.\\n- Do not invent facts or behaviors not present in `{rule}`.\\n\\nCondition handling\\n- If `conditionType` == \\\"Simple\\\" (case-insensitive), assemble a human-readable condition from `whenConditions`:\\n  • Map operators to natural phrases: EqualTo → \\\"is\\\", NotEqualTo → \\\"is not\\\", Contains → \\\"contains\\\", NotContains → \\\"does not contain\\\", GreaterThan → \\\"is greater than\\\", GreaterThanOrEqualTo → \\\"is at least\\\", LessThan → \\\"is less than\\\", LessThanOrEqualTo → \\\"is at most\\\", In/AnyOf → \\\"is one of [A, B]\\\", NotIn → \\\"is not any of [A, B]\\\".\\n  • For list values, join them with commas: `\\\"Color\\\" is one of Red, Blue`.\\n  • Combine multiple `whenConditions` using `whenConditionsGrouping`: `\\\"1\\\"/\\\"AND\\\"/\\\"and\\\"` → join with \\\"and\\\"; `\\\"2\\\"/\\\"OR\\\"/\\\"or\\\"` → join with \\\"or\\\"; default to \\\"and\\\" if missing or unrecognized.\\n  • Quote attribute names, do not quote simple unambiguous values unless necessary for clarity.\\n\\n- If `conditionType` == \\\"Always True\\\" (case-insensitive), **do not** produce the phrase \\\"When always true\\\". Instead create a direct action statement that does **not** begin with \\\"When\\\". Append ` (Always True case)` at the end of that sentence to make the Always-True nature explicit. Example: `Recommended Items: 2 partA and 2 partB are mandatory. (Always True case)`\\n\\nAction wording & descriptive content\\n- Make the sentence(s) informative: include what triggers the rule (for Simple conditions), which attributes/items are affected, the effective action, and any visible outcome or scope you can derive from fields in the JSON (for example: whether price is added to total, whether the price is displayed, whether the recommendation is forced, whether attributes are hidden).\\n- Keep language non-technical and factual; do not include internal variable names, error messages, or implementation details.\\n\\nSpecific rule-type behaviors (must follow these precisely)\\n- **Constraint**: Describe condition and the constraint effect. Use words like \\\"require\\\", \\\"must\\\", \\\"prevent\\\", \\\"disallow\\\" as appropriate based only on fields present. Example: `Constraint: When \\\"ProductType\\\" is Laptop and \\\"Memory\\\" is less than 8GB, require \\\"Memory\\\" to be at least 8GB.`\\n\\n- **Recommendation** / **Recommended Items**:\\n  • If `mandateRecommendedItems` == \\\"Y\\\" or true:\\n      - If `conditionType` is Simple: output one descriptive sentence in the form:\\n        `<ExactRuleType>: When [condition], mandates [N] <itemA> and [M] <itemB> (list items or recommended values).`\\n      - If `conditionType` is Always True: **do not** use \\\"When always true\\\" — instead output:\\n        `<ExactRuleType>: [list items] are mandatory. (Always True case)`\\n  • If `mandateRecommendedItems` == \\\"N\\\" or false or missing:\\n      - If `conditionType` is Simple: output:\\n        `<ExactRuleType>: When [condition], recommends [items/values].`\\n      - If `conditionType` is Always True:\\n        `<ExactRuleType>: [list items] are recommended. (Always True case)`\\n  • If `set_type` indicates forced behavior (e.g., \\\"Forced Set\\\"), include a short parenthetical tag \\\"(forced)\\\" in the same sentence when a specific attribute/value is set. Example: `recommends \\\"GPU\\\" = HighEnd (forced).`\\n\\n- **Hiding**: State which attributes/items are hidden and when. If Always True, produce a direct sentence like:\\n  `<ExactRuleType>: [items] are hidden. (Always True case)`\\n  For Simple conditions: `<ExactRuleType>: When [condition], hide [items].`\\n\\n- **BOM Mapping**: State mapping clearly. For Simple conditions:\\n  `<ExactRuleType>: When [condition], map to BOM item [ID]` and include \\\"(based on data table)\\\" or \\\"(based on script)\\\" only if the JSON indicates table-based or script-based mapping.\\n\\n- **Pricing**: Summarize price behavior and visibility:\\n  e.g., `<ExactRuleType>: When [condition], set price to $X and add it to the total but do not display it to the user.` Use `priceAmount`, `addPriceToTotalPrice`, `displayPriceToUser`, `hideZeroPriceFromUser` to form concise readable statements.\\n\\nAlways-true rule handling (global rule)\\n- NEVER use the phrase `When always true`. Instead:\\n  • For action-only descriptions (no condition) produce a direct action sentence.\\n  • Append ` (Always True case)` to the sentence to flag it is unconditional.\\n\\nQuoting and punctuation\\n- Attribute names: always wrapped in ASCII straight double quotes (`\\\"`), e.g., `\\\"ProductType\\\"`.\\n- Values: avoid quoting simple numbers or unambiguous strings; quote only when necessary for clarity and use straight quotes.\\n- Do not wrap the entire output in quotes or add extra punctuation beyond normal sentence punctuation.\\n\\nLength and tone\\n- Be descriptive: aim for 1–3 short sentences that clearly state trigger (if present), action, affected items, and visible outcome.\\n- No extra commentary, no maker notes, and no invented information.\\n\\nEXAMPLES (use exact ruleType values shown below as prefixes)\\n\\n1) Constraint (Simple, AND)\\nInput:\\n{\\n  \\\"ruleType\\\":\\\"Constraint\\\",\\n  \\\"conditionType\\\":\\\"Simple\\\",\\n  \\\"whenConditions\\\":[\\n    {\\\"attribute\\\":\\\"ProductType\\\",\\\"operator\\\":\\\"EqualTo\\\",\\\"value\\\":\\\"Laptop\\\"},\\n    {\\\"attribute\\\":\\\"Memory\\\",\\\"operator\\\":\\\"LessThan\\\",\\\"value\\\":\\\"8GB\\\"}\\n  ],\\n  \\\"whenConditionsGrouping\\\":\\\"1\\\",\\n  \\\"thenActions\\\":[{\\\"attribute\\\":\\\"Memory\\\",\\\"operator\\\":\\\"GreaterThanOrEqualTo\\\",\\\"value\\\":\\\"8GB\\\"}]\\n}\\nOutput:\\nConstraint: When \\\"ProductType\\\" is Laptop and \\\"Memory\\\" is less than 8GB, require \\\"Memory\\\" to be at least 8GB, preventing configurations with insufficient memory.\\n\\n2) Recommendation (Recommended Items) — mandate = true, Simple condition\\nInput:\\n{\\n  \\\"ruleType\\\":\\\"Recommended Items\\\",\\n  \\\"conditionType\\\":\\\"Simple\\\",\\n  \\\"whenConditions\\\":[{\\\"attribute\\\":\\\"Usage\\\",\\\"operator\\\":\\\"EqualTo\\\",\\\"value\\\":\\\"ServerBuild\\\"}],\\n  \\\"recommendedItems\\\":[\\\"RAIDController\\\",\\\"BatteryBackup\\\"],\\n  \\\"mandateRecommendedItems\\\":\\\"Y\\\"\\n}\\nOutput:\\nRecommended Items: When \\\"Usage\\\" is ServerBuild, mandates recommended items RAIDController and BatteryBackup to ensure required redundancy and backup capability for server builds.\\n\\n3) Recommendation (Recommended Items) — mandate = true, Always True\\nInput:\\n{\\n  \\\"ruleType\\\":\\\"Recommended Items\\\",\\n  \\\"conditionType\\\":\\\"Always True\\\",\\n  \\\"recommendedItems\\\":[\\\"PartA\\\",\\\"PartB\\\"],\\n  \\\"mandateRecommendedItems\\\":\\\"Y\\\"\\n}\\nOutput:\\nRecommended Items: PartA and PartB are mandatory. (Always True case)\\n\\n4) Recommendation — mandate = false, Simple condition with set_type forced\\nInput:\\n{\\n  \\\"ruleType\\\":\\\"Recommendation\\\",\\n  \\\"conditionType\\\":\\\"Simple\\\",\\n  \\\"whenConditions\\\":[{\\\"attribute\\\":\\\"Usage\\\",\\\"operator\\\":\\\"EqualTo\\\",\\\"value\\\":\\\"Gaming\\\"}],\\n  \\\"thenActions\\\":[{\\\"attribute\\\":\\\"GPU\\\",\\\"value\\\":\\\"HighEnd\\\"}],\\n  \\\"set_type\\\":\\\"Forced Set\\\",\\n  \\\"mandateRecommendedItems\\\":\\\"N\\\"\\n}\\nOutput:\\nRecommendation: When \\\"Usage\\\" is Gaming, recommends \\\"GPU\\\" = HighEnd (forced), automatically setting the high-performance GPU for gaming configurations.\\n\\n5) Hiding — Always True\\nInput:\\n{\\n  \\\"ruleType\\\":\\\"Hiding\\\",\\n  \\\"conditionType\\\":\\\"Always True\\\",\\n  \\\"itemsToHide\\\":[\\\"PromoBanner\\\"]\\n}\\nOutput:\\nHiding: \\\"PromoBanner\\\" is hidden from the configuration UI. (Always True case)\\n\\n6) Pricing — Simple\\nInput:\\n{\\n  \\\"ruleType\\\":\\\"Pricing\\\",\\n  \\\"conditionType\\\":\\\"Simple\\\",\\n  \\\"whenConditions\\\":[{\\\"attribute\\\":\\\"Model\\\",\\\"operator\\\":\\\"EqualTo\\\",\\\"value\\\":\\\"ProX\\\"}],\\n  \\\"priceAmount\\\":\\\"150\\\",\\n  \\\"addPriceToTotalPrice\\\":\\\"Y\\\",\\n  \\\"displayPriceToUser\\\":\\\"N\\\"\\n}\\nOutput:\\nPricing: When \\\"Model\\\" is ProX, set price to $150 and add it to the total, but do not display the additional charge to the user.\\n\\nACCEPTANCE CHECKLIST\\n- Output begins with the exact `ruleType` value followed by a colon and a space.\\n- Attribute names are wrapped in ASCII straight double quotes.\\n- ConditionType == Always True never produces the phrase \\\"When always true\\\"; instead the description is direct and ends with ` (Always True case)`.\\n- `mandateRecommendedItems` is handled exactly as specified.\\n- Descriptions are descriptive (1–3 short sentences), factual, and use only fields present in the JSON.\\n- No invented information, no internal variables, and no extra commentary."
            }
          ],
          "outputSpecification": "",
          "type": "LLM"
        },
        {
          "metadata": {
            "modelConfiguration": {
              "modelProperties": {
                "k": 0,
                "max_completion_tokens": 32000,
                "reasoning_effort": "low"
              },
              "code": "ORA_MODEL_CONFIG_PREMIUM_OPEN_AI_GPT_4_1_MINI",
              "model": "OCI_GPT_5_MINI",
              "modelName": "Gpt-5 Mini",
              "provider": "CERT_PREMIUM"
            },
            "name": "Generate Commerce Rule Description LLM"
          },
          "outcomes": {
            "success": "end"
          },
          "code": "GENERATE_COMMERCE_RULE_DESCRIPTION_LLM",
          "id": "1772490238260",
          "inputs": [
            {
              "id": "1772490238261_1",
              "name": "prompt",
              "type": "string",
              "value": "You are an expert Oracle CPQ administrator creating Commerce rule descriptions for business users. Oracle CPQ Commerce rules (Constraint, Validation, Access) enforce business logic in the quoting process at the main document (e.g., quote header) or sub-document (e.g., line items) level. Rules use AJAX for real-time updates and can affect attributes across levels. Sub-document rules only impact sub-document attributes.\\n\\nRule Types Explanation:\\n\\nConstraint Rules: Restrict attribute values when a condition is met (e.g., block discounts for specific customers). The sentence should describe the constraint outcome first, then the condition(s).\\n\\nValidation Rules: Ensure valid data before actions like submitting a quote (e.g., require a field to be non-empty). The sentence should state the required action first (e.g., “must be provided”), then the condition(s).\\n\\nAccess Rules: Hide, disable, or make attributes read-only when conditions are met (e.g., hide fields based on part numbers). The sentence should state the condition(s) first, then the outcome (e.g., “hidden” or “disabled” for non-boolean fields, “read-only” for boolean fields).\\n\\nInstructions\\n\\nGiven a JSON object {{$context.$triggers.REST.$input.ruleInstance}} representing an Oracle CPQ rule with the following relevant structure:\\n\\nruleType: The type of rule (e.g., “Constraint”, “Validation”, “Access”).\\n\\n <ExactRuleTypeFromJSON>: [Sentence 1] [Sentence 2 if needed]\\n\\nImportant: Use the rule type **exactly** as provided in the JSON field `ruleType` (case and wording unchanged). Do not modify, normalize, or translate the ruleType in any way. If `ruleType` is missing, use the literal text `Rule` as a fallback.\\n\\nGeneral formatting rules\\n- Always start the output with the exact `ruleType` value followed by a colon and a single space.\\n- Always wrap **attribute names** in ASCII straight double quotes `\\\"` (for example `\\\"ProductType\\\"`).\\n- Use ASCII straight double quotes only — never use typographic (curly) quotes.\\n- Output must be plain text only — no JSON, markdown, or extra commentary.\\n- Do not invent facts or behaviors not present in `{rule}`.\\n\\n\\nconditionType: Either \\\"Simple\\\" or \\\"Always True\\\". If \\\"Always True\\\", do not include the whenConditions array and only summarize the thenActions.\\n\\nwhenConditions: An array of one or more condition objects. Each condition has:\\n\\nattribute_label: The user-friendly field name (e.g., “Valid Stages for Current Process”, “Stage”, “Has Recurring Pricing”).\\n\\nA comparison operator (e.g., “contains”, “not contains”, “is”, “is not”) and one or more values.\\n\\nwhenConditionsGrouping: A string indicating how to combine multiple conditions (e.g., \\\"1 OR 2\\\", \\\"1 AND 2\\\"). Explain how the numbers correspond to the elements in whenConditions.\\n\\nthenActions: An array of action objects. Each action has:\\n\\nattribute_label: The field that is affected by the rule (e.g., “Quote for Agreement”, “Discount”, “Annual Value (Net)”). Display attributes in quotes.\\n\\ncond_type: The rule type again (e.g., “access”, “constraint”).\\n\\nPossibly a flag or operator indicating what to do (e.g., hide, disable, constrain to a range or value).\\n\\nlinkedActions: If present, the rule should trigger only on these linked actions.\\n\\nOutput Requirements\\n\\nGenerate one sentence describing the rule in plain, business-friendly language, following these conventions:\\n\\nUse only the condition values and action details from the JSON. Do not use the rule name or message field. Use attribute_label values for clarity (convert technical field names into spaced words). Avoid technical jargon or code.\\n\\nMultiple conditions should be combined correctly:\\n\\nIf whenConditionsGrouping is OR (e.g., \\\"1 OR 2\\\"), use “or” (e.g., “either X or Y …”).\\n\\nIf AND (e.g., \\\"1 AND 2\\\"), use “and” (e.g., “when X and Y …”).\\n\\nFor each condition, use language like “is”, “contains”, “is not”, “is over/under” based on the operator.\\n\\nIf conditionType is \\\"Always True\\\", skip conditions and summarize only the thenActions.\\n\\nIf linked actions are present, mention that the rule applies only to those linked actions.\\n\\nFuture tense: Summarize thenActions as actions that will occur.\\n\\nFollow the style of the examples below exactly.\\n\\nInput/Output Examples\\n\\nInput:\\n\\n{\\\"ruleType\\\":\\\"Constraint\\\", \\\"whenConditions\\\":[{\\\"attribute_label\\\":\\\"Bill To Company\\\", \\\"operator\\\":\\\"is\\\", \\\"value\\\":\\\"Acme\\\"}], \\\"thenActions\\\":[{\\\"attribute_label\\\":\\\"Discount\\\", \\\"cond_type\\\":\\\"constraint\\\", \\\"constrain\\\":\\\">0\\\"}]}\\n\\n\\nOutput: Discounts will not be applied when \\\"Bill To Company\\\" is \\\"Acme\\\".\\n\\nInput:\\n\\n{\\\"ruleType\\\":\\\"Validation\\\", \\\"whenConditions\\\":[{\\\"attribute_label\\\":\\\"Quote Status\\\", \\\"operator\\\":\\\"is\\\", \\\"value\\\":\\\"Pending Approval\\\"}], \\\"thenActions\\\":[{\\\"attribute_label\\\":\\\"Approval Reason\\\", \\\"cond_type\\\":\\\"constraint\\\", \\\"validate\\\":\\\"not empty\\\"}]}\\n\\n\\nOutput: \\\"Approval Reason\\\" will need to be provided when \\\"Quote Status\\\" is \\\"Pending Approval\\\".\\n\\nInput:\\n\\n{\\\"ruleType\\\":\\\"Access\\\", \\\"whenConditions\\\":[{\\\"attribute_label\\\":\\\"Has Recurring Pricing\\\", \\\"operator\\\":\\\"is\\\", \\\"value\\\":\\\"false\\\"}], \\\"thenActions\\\":[{\\\"attribute_label\\\":\\\"Contracted Periods\\\", \\\"cond_type\\\":\\\"access\\\", \\\"hide\\\":true}, {\\\"attribute_label\\\":\\\"Annual Value (Net)\\\", \\\"cond_type\\\":\\\"access\\\", \\\"hide\\\":true}]}\\n\\n\\nOutput: When \\\"Has Recurring Pricing\\\" is \\\"false\\\", \\\"Contracted Periods\\\" and \\\"Annual Value (Net)\\\" will be hidden.\\n\\nTask\\n\\nWrite the one-sentence rule description for the provided {rule} JSON object, following the format, tone, and all conventions described above."
            }
          ],
          "type": "LLM"
        }
      ],
